//? Title: Wasserstein GAN: Clipping and an Input-Gradient Penalty
//? Subtitle: Two explicit native training schedules on the same ordered Gaussian ring
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Two small Wasserstein games fit the same400 points from eight noisy two-dimensional
ring components. Each2→16 tanh→2 generator has82 optimized values; its2→16 tanh→1
critic has65. The clipped variant uses RMSProp and clips every critic tensor,
including biases, before fitting and after each critic write. The penalty variant
uses Adam and differentiates the actual critic input Jacobian through critic
parameters. Both take three fresh-noise critic steps on a shared real batch, then
one fresh generator step through the updated critic. The complete native program
keeps every optimizer/clip call visible and reproduces the source artifacts.
Three epochs leave both generated distributions far from the ring. The reported
critic gaps are unconverged game diagnostics, not certified Wasserstein distances.
)
\toc

> Separate the sample map, critic update and input-gradient path

The references are [Arjovsky, Chintala and Bottou, Wasserstein GAN (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.07875)
and [Gulrajani et al., Improved Training of Wasserstein GANs (2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.00028).
This repository uses tiny tanh/linear teaching networks and three critic steps per
batch. It does not reproduce the papers' image-model architectures or benchmarks.
All147 parameters in eight tensors are checkpointed. Two stores and two optimizer
states belong to each independent trial; the trials do not share fitted weights.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wgan_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
