> Check a penalty derivative by hand before fitting anything

For the supplied linear critic c(x)=w^T x with w=(3,4), its input Jacobian isw,
its norm is5 and the unit-norm penalty is(5-1)^2=16. The derivative with respect
to w is2*(5-1)*w/5=(4.8,6.4). This is a derivative through an input derivative;
detaching the Jacobian would lose the parameter path needed by WGAN-GP. The source
uses sqrt(sum(J^2)+1e-12), whose tiny guard is retained below. This toy has no
learned parameters or adversarial updates and is distinct from the tanh critic.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wgan_linear_penalty.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  auto t      = n::DType::Float64;
  auto x = n::ones({2, 2}, t).requires_grad_(), w = n::tensor({3., 4.}, {2, 1}, t).requires_grad_();
  n::GradOptions options;
  options.create_graph = true;
  auto j               = n::grad(n::sum(n::matmul(x, w)), {x}, options)[0];
  auto penalty         = n::mean(n::square(n::sqrt(n::sum(n::square(j), {1}) + 1e-12) - 1.));
  auto dw              = n::grad(penalty, {w})[0];
  if (std::abs(penalty.item() - 16) > 1e-10 || std::abs(dw.at({0, 0}) - 4.8) > 1e-10 ||
      std::abs(dw.at({1, 0}) - 6.4) > 1e-10)
    throw std::runtime_error("linear penalty derivative");
  std::print("Penalty={}, derivative dw={}\n", penalty.item(), dw.values_str());
  std::vector<double> scale, value;
  for (int k = 0; k <= 60; ++k) {
    double a = k * .02;
    scale.push_back(a);
    value.push_back(std::pow(std::sqrt(25 * a * a + 1e-12) - 1, 2));
  }
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(-.5, 40)
      .lines(scale, value, "Supplied w=scale*(3,4)", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .x_label("Scale of the supplied critic weights")
      .y_label("Squared distance of input-gradient norm from1")
      .title("The norm target is1, not a zero gradient")
      .save_svg("build/wgan_linear_penalty.svg");
}
```
