> Compare the norm penalty across the two trials

The clipping trial still reports its gradient-norm penalty even though that term
does not enter its critic objective. Its near-zero Jacobians incur a penalty near1.
The penalty trial reduces this diagnostic over the three epochs, but its final
test penalty about.348145 remains far from zero. A lower norm penalty at sampled
interpolations does not prove global Lipschitz continuity or correct sampling.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/wgan_penalties.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::vector<double> x, clip, gp;
  for (auto variant : {"clipped", "gradient_penalty"}) {
    std::ifstream f(std::filesystem::path("build/wasserstein_gan") / variant / "history.tsv");
    std::string   h;
    std::getline(f, h);
    double e, c, g, gap, p;
    while (f >> e >> c >> g >> gap >> p) {
      if (std::string(variant) == "clipped") {
        x.push_back(e);
        clip.push_back(p);
      } else
        gp.push_back(p);
    }
  }
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.y_limits(0, 1.5)
      .lines(x, clip, "Clipped diagnostic only", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points(x, clip, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .lines(x, gp, "GP fitted coefficient10", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .points(x, gp, "", {220, 38, 38}, 4)
      .x_label("Completed epoch; no early stopping")
      .y_label("Validation mean((input-gradient norm-1)^2)")
      .title("The two trials optimize different critic objectives")
      .save_svg("build/wgan_penalties.svg");
}
